window.MANUSCRIPT_VERSIONS = [{"v": 0, "date": "2026-09-22 10:46", "note": "original manuscript2_html", "html": "\n<h1>Online estimation of the NCF stability indicator</h1>\n<p>This section derives the normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) for the outer controller loop. The estimator combines three branches: direct identification from displacement measurements, residual output-power inference, and a healthy-model prior. The following formulation develops these branches and combines their contributions into a single indicator for adaptive control.</p><h2>1. Outer controller loop and NCF margin</h2><p>Figure 1 shows the outer controller loop, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is the desired displacement obtained from the numerical substructure after phase-lead compensation. The signal <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is the command sent by the controller to the transfer system, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. The controller is denoted by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> is the Laplace variable. The plant <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> represents the transfer system and physical substructure. It includes the actuator and servo-valve dynamics, together with control–structure interaction. The parameter <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> denotes the specimen stiffness.</p><p>The adaptive factor <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math> scales the controller output. The adaptive controller automatically updates <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math> based on the online stability indicator to maintain stable operation and tracking performance. The open-loop dynamics are given by</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div><p>The plant relates the control command to the measured displacement as</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div><p>Here, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, respectively. Hence, the closed-loop response is</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div><p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is the Laplace transform of the desired displacement.</p><figure id=\"fig1\"><svg aria-labelledby=\"loop-title loop-desc\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0 0 1000 280\">\n<title id=\"loop-title\">Outer controller loop for NCF OSI estimation</title>\n<desc id=\"loop-desc\">Desired displacement u enters the summing junction. The controller C produces an output scaled by adaptive factor lambda. The resulting command v drives the transfer system and physical substructure H. Measured actuator displacement y returns to the summing junction.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"loop-arrow\" markerheight=\"7\" markerwidth=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\" refx=\"9\" refy=\"5\" viewbox=\"0 0 10 10\"><path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 Z\" fill=\"#333\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\">\n<path d=\"M 28 108 H 124\" marker-end=\"url(#loop-arrow)\"></path><circle cx=\"148\" cy=\"108\" r=\"23\"></circle>\n<path d=\"M 171 108 H 226\" marker-end=\"url(#loop-arrow)\"></path>\n<rect height=\"78\" width=\"153\" x=\"228\" y=\"69\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M 381 108 H 426\" marker-end=\"url(#loop-arrow)\"></path><rect height=\"78\" width=\"130\" x=\"428\" y=\"69\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M 558 108 H 626\" marker-end=\"url(#loop-arrow)\"></path><rect height=\"118\" width=\"250\" x=\"628\" y=\"49\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M 878 108 H 979\" marker-end=\"url(#loop-arrow)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M 915 108 V 224 H 148 V 132\" marker-end=\"url(#loop-arrow)\"></path>\n</g><circle cx=\"915\" cy=\"108\" fill=\"#333\" r=\"3.5\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\"><text font-style=\"italic\" x=\"71\" y=\"88\">u(t)</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"104\">+</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"122\">−</text>\n<text font-style=\"italic\" x=\"304\" y=\"101\">C(s)</text><text class=\"note\" x=\"304\" y=\"126\">Controller</text>\n<text font-style=\"italic\" x=\"493\" y=\"101\">λ</text><text class=\"note\" x=\"493\" y=\"126\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text font-style=\"italic\" x=\"587\" y=\"88\">v(t)</text><text font-style=\"italic\" x=\"753\" y=\"83\">H(s, k)</text>\n<text x=\"753\" y=\"114\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"753\" y=\"142\">and physical substructure</text>\n<text font-style=\"italic\" x=\"946\" y=\"88\">y(t)</text><text class=\"note\" x=\"532\" y=\"251\">Measured actuator displacement</text></g></svg>\n<figcaption>Figure 1. Outer controller loop. The adaptive factor scales the controller output before the command is sent to the transfer system and physical substructure.</figcaption></figure><p>The NCF margin measures robustness to perturbations in a normalized coprime factor description <a href=\"#citation1\">[1]</a>. For an internally stable interconnection of a general plant <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and controller <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, the margin is defined by</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">‖</mo><mo>[</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>[</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∞</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div><p>Here, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> is the identity matrix and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo><mo>·</mo><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo><mo>∞</mo></msub></mrow></math> is the <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∞</mo></msub></mrow></math> norm, defined as the largest singular value maximized over frequency. Following the SISO derivation presented by Gomaa et al. <a href=\"#citation2\">[2]</a>, this expression becomes</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div><p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></math> is angular frequency and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is the imaginary unit. This form avoids a numerical singular-value calculation. For the present indicator, the forward path is grouped into <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The resulting margin is</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msub><mi>δ</mi><mo fence=\"true\" maxsize=\"1.2em\" minsize=\"1.2em\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" maxsize=\"1.2em\" minsize=\"1.2em\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div><p>At a given angular frequency, the substitution <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></math> gives the complex open-loop response <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. This value contains both the magnitude and phase of the loop response. The function <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> is the normalized chordal distance between this response and the critical point <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div><p>As the open-loop response approaches <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the numerator approaches zero. Consequently, a smaller value of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> indicates a smaller stability margin. The three estimator branches apply the following common mapping to their respective loop-response estimates:</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div><h2>2. Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>, using the latest <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> of displacement data. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> index the update and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> index the samples within that window. The number of samples is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency. Denote the current segments by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. Their windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>Here, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> is the frequency-bin index and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The Hann window is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math>. Windowing reduces the influence of discontinuities at the segment boundaries. Averaging windowed spectral estimates follows the modified-periodogram approach introduced by Welch <a href=\"#citation3\">[3]</a>.</p>\n<p>At each frequency, the instantaneous input power and output power are accompanied by the cross-power term:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The superscript <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>*</mo></mrow></math> denotes complex conjugation. These are unnormalized spectral powers. The common normalization cancels in the ratios used below. To reduce fluctuations between successive windows, each term is updated through an exponentially weighted average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>An overbar denotes the averaged estimate. The smoothing coefficient is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.39</mn></mrow></math>, and the first complete window initializes each estimate. Exponential averaging assigns progressively smaller weights to older observations <a href=\"#citation4\">[4]</a>. It therefore provides a short memory while allowing the spectra to follow changes in the experiment.</p>\n<p>For <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains 1024 samples and the bin spacing is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. The retained frequency set is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>Excluding the line-noise interval leaves 47 frequency bins. In the following equations, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></math> denotes a frequency at which the estimator is evaluated. Spectra are written as functions of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></math>, while transfer-function values are written at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></math>. The subscript <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> identifies the current update and its operating condition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the current, generally unknown stiffness. Thus, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> estimates the current response <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>; no separate estimate of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is required.</p>\n<h2>3. Confidence-weighted identification</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give a measured estimate of the closed-loop response and its magnitude-squared coherence:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The hat denotes an estimate. Coherence lies between zero and one and measures the consistency of the linear command–response relationship. Interpreting the spectral ratio as <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with the command. This assumption requires care in RTHS because the command may itself be influenced by feedback through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore supporting evidence, rather than proof of an unbiased estimate; feedback-related identification bias is discussed by Forssell and Ljung <a href=\"#citation5\">[5]</a>.</p>\n<p>The confidence assigned to this estimate also depends on command activity. Define the root-mean-square command amplitude <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and a binary activity variable <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>The activity threshold <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> has the same displacement units as <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Its selected value is 0.0575 in the recorded displacement units, approximately <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation estimated from a quiet record.</p>\n<p>Within an active window, a frequency bin may still contain little command energy. Its relative input power <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and input leverage <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are defined as</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">B</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz analysis grid, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>ν</mi></mrow></math> is a frequency on that grid, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is a dimensionless reference level. The leverage suppresses the influence of bins with weak input power. Combining these measures gives the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>For a bin with vanishing input power, the measured ratio is not used and its confidence is taken as zero. The confidence is a weighting factor, not a statistical confidence probability.</p>\n<p>When measured information is limited, the estimate is drawn toward the healthy loop model. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> denote the healthy full-gain loop, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> is the initial specimen stiffness. At update <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the adaptive factor <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> gives the prior responses</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>The subscript <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></math> denotes the model prior. In particular, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is the healthy-model response at the current adaptive factor. The usable closed-loop estimate is the confidence-weighted combination</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>The tilde denotes the blended response. The reconstructed open-loop estimate follows by solving Equation (3) for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. Thus, a well-supported measurement governs the estimate, while a weakly supported measurement leaves it close to the prior.</p>\n<p>The identified margin curve <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is then obtained from Equation (8). The corresponding prior curve is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>The operator <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a moving median over three adjacent frequency bins. It reduces isolated spectral irregularities. Both curves remain functions of frequency until the evidence is combined.</p>\n<h2>4. Residual output-power inference</h2>\n<p>Weak command excitation limits direct identification, but the measured response can retain evidence of a change in the loop. To establish a reference, the first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent healthy operation at full gain. Their median output power is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>median</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The index <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math> labels the reference windows, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is the windowed transform of the measured displacement in reference window <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. The fixed reference power is denoted by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The observed ratio <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> measures subsequent changes relative to it.</p>\n<p>When the desired displacement contributes little to the measured response, an equivalent additive output disturbance is filtered by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. If the disturbance spectrum remains approximately unchanged from the healthy reference, a candidate loop <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> predicts the output-power ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>The positive, dimensionless factor <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> represents an effective change in loop magnitude. It is not a direct estimate of specimen stiffness. Equation (32) also shows the assumption behind the inference: changes in disturbance intensity or command-driven response can produce output-power changes that are unrelated to stability.</p>\n<p>At each retained frequency, a candidate is selected by matching the observed and modeled power ratios in logarithmic form:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo></mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>Here, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math> contains 25 candidate values between 0.5 and 6, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>log</mi></mrow></math> denotes the natural logarithm. Logarithmic comparison measures relative discrepancies between positive power ratios. The fit is performed independently at each frequency, so the resulting <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> curve is an inference model rather than a uniquely identified physical transfer function.</p>\n<p>The fitted change is retained through a logarithmic memory. Denote the remembered value by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">―</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, initialized at one, and let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> be the target of the current update. The remembered value is updated as an exponentially weighted average in logarithmic space:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">―</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">―</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>During accumulation of residual evidence, the target is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and the update coefficient is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.12</mn></mrow></math>. Recovery is assessed using the band-median observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>median</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>When <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the recovery target is one and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.27</mn></mrow></math>. The remembered change then decays toward its healthy value. In the absence of a fitting or recovery update, it is held. This memory allows previously detected changes to remain represented during periods of weak excitation.</p>\n<p>The residual margin curve is evaluated using the same distance as the identified branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq36\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">―</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(36)</span></div>\n<p>The subscript <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></math> denotes residual output-power inference. Using a common distance permits the two branches to be combined at the same frequency.</p>\n<h2>5. Evidence fusion and reported NCF OSI</h2>\n<p>The contribution of the residual branch depends on the size of the inferred change. Define its detection weight <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq37\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(37)</span></div>\n<p>The selected detection scale is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.75</mn></mrow></math>. The clipping operator limits its argument to the stated interval. The weight increases continuously from zero at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">―</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at the selected detection scale <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. This scale determines how strongly an inferred loop change contributes to the indicator.</p>\n<p>The confidence <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> assigns weight to direct identification. The residual branch receives part of the remaining weight, with any unsupported share assigned to the prior:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq38\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(38)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq39\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(39)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq40\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(40)</span></div>\n<p>These three nonnegative weights sum to one. At each retained frequency, the combined distance estimate is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq41\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtable columnalign=\"left\" rowspacing=\"0.65em\"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(41)</span></div>\n<p>All terms on the right-hand side are evaluated at the same frequency. The symbol <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> distinguishes this combined curve from the final scalar indicator. When command activity vanishes, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, but remembered residual evidence may still contribute. If neither branch supplies evidence of a change, the estimate returns to the current gain-scaled prior. Such a value remains model-supported rather than newly measured.</p>\n<p>Frequency reduction is performed after fusion. Sort the 47 retained values of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> in ascending order and denote the second-smallest value by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The instantaneous indicator is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq42\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(42)</span></div>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the selected frequency. This reduction discards one lowest value so that a single low bin does not govern the complete estimate. It is a deliberate robustness choice in the estimator, and it can also suppress a genuine narrow frequency minimum.</p>\n<p>Finally, the reported NCF OSI is obtained by smoothing the instantaneous value in time:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq43\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(43)</span></div>\n<p>The coefficient <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math> specifies the fraction of the newest estimate admitted at each update. Temporal smoothing reduces fluctuations, but introduces a delay in following abrupt changes.</p>\n<p>The resulting NCF OSI tracks changes in the stability margin within the monitored frequency band. Its interpretation depends on the available excitation and the healthy-model prior. Residual inference also depends on the assumed disturbance spectrum. Frequency filtering and the removal of one lowest value mean that the reported indicator is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency NCF margin. The adaptive controller uses this indicator to update <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> during the experiment.</p>\n<section aria-labelledby=\"references-title\" class=\"references\">\n<h2 id=\"references-title\">References</h2>\n<ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H∞-bounded uncertainty,” <em>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</em>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a>.</li><li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An Online Stability Indicator for Real-Time Hybrid Simulation Emphasizing Physical Substructure Degradation,” author manuscript, Section 4.</li><li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <em>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</em>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a>.</li><li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <em>Technometrics</em>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a>.</li><li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <em>Automatica</em>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a>.</li></ol>\n</section>\n"}, {"v": 1, "date": "2026-09-23 14:26", "note": "journal redesign with review edits", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) introduced in the authors’ previous study [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the actuator control loop in hybrid simulation to degradation of the specimen. This section describes how the indicator is estimated during the test from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement. Three sources of evidence are combined at each frequency. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, of the closed loop from the measured signals. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which detects a change in the loop from the change in measured output power. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, obtained before the test. Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the test from the estimated indicator. The derivation of the SISO margin and its relation to specimen damage are given in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] and are restated here only as required by the estimator.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the closed-loop estimate and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 2, "date": "2026-09-23 15:20", "note": "applied v1 review comments 1-4", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. That study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification rather than a time delay. A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. This section describes how the indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement. To keep the indicator reliable when the excitation is weak or changing, three sources of evidence are combined at each frequency. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response function of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. When the loop changes at a given frequency, this branch detects that change by comparing the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, obtained before the experiment. Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the experiment from the estimated indicator. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the following sections.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 3, "date": "2026-09-23 17:16", "note": "edited in the review page", "author": "user", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. That study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification rather than a time delay. A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. This section describes how the indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement. To keep the indicator reliable when the excitation is weak or changing, three sources of evidence are combined at each frequency. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response function of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. When the loop changes at a given frequency, this branch detects that change by comparing the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, obtained before the experiment. Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the experiment from the estimated indicator. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the following sections. hi</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"></rect><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"></circle>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>δ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"></rect><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 4, "date": "2026-09-23 17:17", "note": "edited in the review page", "author": "user", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. That study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification rather than a time delay. A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. This section describes how the indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement. To keep the indicator reliable when the excitation is weak or changing, three sources of evidence are combined at each frequency. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response function of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. When the loop changes at a given frequency, this branch detects that change by comparing the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, obtained before the experiment. Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the experiment from the estimated indicator. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the following sections.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"></rect><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"></circle>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>δ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"></rect><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 5, "date": "2026-09-23 17:42", "note": "Saved from the review page", "author": "user", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. That study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification rather than a time delay. A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. This section describes how the indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement. To keep the indicator reliable when the excitation is weak or changing, three sources of evidence are combined at each frequency. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response function of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. When the loop changes at a given frequency, this branch detects that change by comparing the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, obtained before the experiment. Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the experiment from the estimated indicator. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the following sections.<span data-user-edit=\"\"> hi</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"></rect><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"></circle>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>δ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"></rect><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 6, "date": "2026-09-23 17:46", "note": "Recovery copy before reverting to v2", "author": "user", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. That study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification rather than a time delay. A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. This section describes how the indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement. To keep the indicator reliable when the excitation is weak or changing, three sources of evidence are combined at each frequency. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response function of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. When the loop changes at a given frequency, this branch detects that change by comparing the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, obtained before the experiment. Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the experiment from the estimated indicator. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the following sections.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 7, "date": "2026-09-23 19:31", "note": "Saved from the review page", "author": "user", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. <span data-user-edit=\"\">The</span> study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification <span data-user-edit=\"\">mechanism.</span> A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. <span data-user-edit=\"\">In</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">this section we extend the analytical study to be implementd experimentally and discuss the experimental challenges and propose how to address them. T</span><span data-user-edit=\"\">he</span> indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator <span data-user-edit=\"\">displacement</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">by</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">combining</span> the <span data-user-edit=\"\">values</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">of</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">three</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">estimator</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">branches</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">at</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">each</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">frequency</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">weighted</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">by</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">our</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">confidence</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">in</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">them.</span> The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop <span data-user-edit=\"\">traansfer</span> of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <span data-user-edit=\"\">bpow,</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">which</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">can</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">detect</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">the</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">transfer</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">function</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">amplifications</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">at</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">frequencies</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">that</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">cannot</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">be</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">reliably</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">identified.&nbsp; bpow</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">compares</span> the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation.<span data-user-edit=\"\">&nbsp;</span>The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, <span data-user-edit=\"\">which</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">cn</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">be</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">used</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">s&nbsp; fallback when no sufficeient information re vailable. </span>Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the experiment from the estimated indicator. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the sections.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.<span data-user-edit=\"\"> hi</span></p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"></rect><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"></circle>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>δ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"></rect><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 8, "date": "2026-09-23 20:07", "note": "applied v6 comments on user v7 (kept user's extension sentence, framework as estimator + adaptive controller)", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. In this section, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The resulting framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that uses it. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated indicator. It scales the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the following sections.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 9, "date": "2026-09-23 20:34", "note": "Saved from the review page", "author": "user", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] <span data-user-edit=\"\">was</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">introduced to quantify </span>the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this section, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The <span data-user-edit=\"\">proposed</span> framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that <span data-user-edit=\"\">gets</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">updated</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">accordingly. </span>The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated <span data-user-edit=\"\">indicator</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">by</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">scaling</span> the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in <span data-user-edit=\"\">this</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">section.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"></rect><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"></circle>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>δ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"></rect><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 10, "date": "2026-09-23 23:27", "note": "rewrote 2.1-2.6 for flow (reason first, known-to-new), kept user's v9 + working-copy lead", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated indicator by scaling the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Before the estimator can be formulated, the loop whose stability it monitors must be defined. Figure 1 shows this loop for a hybrid simulation with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground motion and the measured force. The phase-lead compensator then advances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to offset the lag of the transfer system, which gives the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, so it includes control–structure interaction. Finally, the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> closes the hybrid loop by returning to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The estimator monitors the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, because specimen degradation acts directly on this loop [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To judge how close this loop is to instability, the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is used, as in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. For the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, its general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>The cap is needed because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, so the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. These frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. Capping them removes this spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency, which must be obtained from measured signals while the experiment runs. For this purpose, the estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>Not every bin is useful. The analysis set keeps the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated. The three branches that follow all start from these averaged spectra.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The most direct way to obtain the loop response is to identify it from the reference and the measured response. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because the estimate can be poor, the estimator decides how much to trust it in each bin. This confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement must be replaced by something more reliable, and the estimator uses the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. Using it requires a reference. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain, and they define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, suppose that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and a detected change must persist once the excitation fades. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory must also forget a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>Each frequency now has three margin estimates, and the estimator must decide how much each one counts. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The adaptive controller needs a single number, so the fused curve is reduced over frequency. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 11, "date": "2026-09-23 23:36", "note": "revised 2.1-2.6 for the reader: no writer notes, no whose, phase lead framed by its purpose", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated indicator by scaling the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>The indicator monitors the stability of the hybrid simulation loop shown in Figure 1. In this loop, the numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground motion and the measured force. A phase-lead compensator, designed to improve the tracking of the transfer system, advances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and gives the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The plant therefore includes control–structure interaction. Finally, the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> returns to the numerical substructure and closes the hybrid loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Within this hybrid loop, specimen degradation acts directly on the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore evaluates this loop. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, its open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the distance of this loop from instability is measured by the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]. For the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the general definition of the margin reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Such frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. The cap removes their spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>During the experiment, the loop response needed in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> is unknown and is estimated from the measured signals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The estimator analyses short windows of these signals and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 12, "date": "2026-09-23 23:43", "note": "fixed technical errors found against Paper 1 (outer controller loop, no 'hybrid loop', degradation changes loop dynamics)", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated indicator by scaling the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>To show where the indicator operates, Figure 1 presents the hybrid simulation with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground motion and the measured force. A phase-lead compensator, designed to improve the tracking of the transfer system, advances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and gives the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The plant therefore includes control–structure interaction. Finally, the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> returns to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>When the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the dynamics of the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, change [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore evaluates this loop. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, its open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the distance of this loop from instability is measured by the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]. For the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the general definition of the margin reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Such frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. The cap removes their spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 13, "date": "2026-09-23 23:48", "note": "transfer system includes inner-loop controller and CSI, margin lumped in L stated as a difference from [2]", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated indicator by scaling the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>To show where the indicator operates, Figure 1 presents the hybrid simulation with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground motion and the measured force. A phase-lead compensator, designed to improve the tracking of the transfer system, advances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and gives the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, the servo-valve, the actuator, and the control–structure interaction between the actuator and the specimen. Finally, the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> returns to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>When the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the dynamics of the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, change [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore evaluates this loop. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, its open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the distance of this loop from instability is measured by the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]. Unlike [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], which separates the outer controller from the plant, the whole loop is lumped here into the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The general definition of the margin then reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Such frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. The cap removes their spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 14, "date": "2026-09-24 10:49", "note": "user's rewrite of the first 2.1 paragraphs", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated indicator by scaling the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To offset the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator advances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> applied to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. Control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> returns to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop. The measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>When the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the dynamics of the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, change [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore evaluates this loop. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, its open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the distance of this loop from instability is measured by the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]. Unlike [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], which separates the outer controller from the plant, the whole loop is lumped here into the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The general definition of the margin then reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Such frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. The cap removes their spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 15, "date": "2026-09-24 11:16", "note": "ported the user's unsaved v14 edits exactly", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilize the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop. Whil the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>When the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the dynamics of the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, change [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore evaluates this loop. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, its open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the distance of this loop from instability is measured by the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]. Unlike [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], which separates the outer controller from the plant, the whole loop is lumped here into the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The general definition of the margin then reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Such frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. The cap removes their spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 16, "date": "2026-09-24 11:16", "note": "fixed three slips in the user's edits: utilizes, compensate for, while", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>When the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the dynamics of the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, change [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore evaluates this loop. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, its open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the distance of this loop from instability is measured by the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]. Unlike [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], which separates the outer controller from the plant, the whole loop is lumped here into the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The general definition of the margin then reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Such frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. The cap removes their spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 17, "date": "2026-09-24 11:29", "note": "Clarify loop margin and capped distance after Figure 1; preserve equations", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Specimen degradation reduces stiffness and changes the dynamics of the outer controller loop [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore monitors the stability margin of this loop from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] quantifies the robustness of the loop to perturbations. Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] evaluated this margin by treating the outer controller and the plant separately. Here the complete loop transfer function is assigned to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. This formulation allows the margin to be evaluated directly from the loop response. The NCF margin for this formulation is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> measures the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> at each frequency. The smallest distance over all frequencies determines the margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> therefore indicates proximity to instability. To limit how much larger distances contribute to the reported indicator, all branches of the estimator use the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>The identity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> shows that the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The cap limits the contribution from these frequencies while preserving smaller distances that indicate proximity to the critical point. It therefore bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> without altering those smaller distances.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 18, "date": "2026-09-24 11:30", "note": "Remove repetition from capped-distance explanation; preserve equations", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Specimen degradation reduces stiffness and changes the dynamics of the outer controller loop [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore monitors the stability margin of this loop from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] quantifies the robustness of the loop to perturbations. Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] evaluated this margin by treating the outer controller and the plant separately. Here the complete loop transfer function is assigned to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. This formulation allows the margin to be evaluated directly from the loop response. The NCF margin for this formulation is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> measures the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> at each frequency. The smallest distance over all frequencies determines the margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> therefore indicates proximity to instability. To limit how much larger distances contribute to the reported indicator, all branches of the estimator use the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>The identity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> shows that the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The cap limits the contribution from these frequencies while preserving smaller distances that indicate proximity to the critical point. It therefore bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 19, "date": "2026-09-24 12:21", "note": "Tighten prose after Figure 1 and remove repeated loop rationale", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>For the outer controller loop in Figure 1, define the plant transfer function as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] treated the outer controller and plant separately. For this calculation, we assign the complete loop to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and use unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to evaluate the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] from the loop response:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> measures the chordal distance from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> is the smallest such distance over all frequencies. To limit the contribution of larger distances, all estimator branches use the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>The identity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> shows that the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The cap limits these larger values while preserving smaller distances to the critical point.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 20, "date": "2026-09-24 12:43", "note": "Restore connected impersonal prose after Figure 1", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, respectively. For the NCF calculation, Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] represented the outer controller and plant separately. In the present formulation, the complete outer controller loop is represented as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] can therefore be evaluated from the loop response:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>In <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>, the frequency infimum of the chordal distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> identifies the closest approach of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A smaller margin therefore indicates greater proximity to instability. Because large distances do not signal proximity to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, they are capped in every estimator branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>The identity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> shows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The cap limits these larger distances while preserving smaller distances to the critical point.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 21, "date": "2026-09-24 13:03", "note": "Use controller–plant NCF margin and remove capped distance", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With the adaptive factor included in the outer controller, the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the plant response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be inferred from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 22, "date": "2026-09-24 13:10", "note": "Address equation comments and revise spectral-estimation prose", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The plant response changes as the specimen degrades, so the margin in Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> is estimated from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> during the experiment. The estimator updates every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> using the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of these signals. Consecutive windows therefore overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, giving a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexing its samples, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The resulting instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce single-window variability, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator monitors the 2–100 Hz band and excludes frequencies around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 23, "date": "2026-09-24 13:37", "note": "Introduce short-time spectral estimation before its implementation details", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Short-time spectral estimation forms auto- and cross-spectra from recent records of the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To keep the spectral estimates responsive to evolving outer controller loop dynamics as the specimen degrades, each update uses a short recent record. These spectra support the subsequent identification of the outer controller loop response and estimation of the margin in Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>. This subsection defines the windowed transforms, recursive averaging, and frequency bins used to form them.</p>\n<p>The spectra are updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> using the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of both signals, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and has a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexing its samples, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 24, "date": "2026-09-24 19:56", "note": "Explain the need for spectral estimation and remove suspended hyphens", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Spectral identification of the outer controller loop's closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight applied to the response estimate at each frequency. As the specimen changes during the experiment, these spectra are formed from short recent records to help the estimates reflect the current operating condition.</p>\n<p>The spectra are updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> using the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of both signals, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and has a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexing its samples, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 25, "date": "2026-09-24 20:13", "note": "Replace framework overview and control-loop paragraphs with author revisions", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI), introduced by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop. In contrast, the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> feeds back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Spectral identification of the outer controller loop's closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight applied to the response estimate at each frequency. As the specimen changes during the experiment, these spectra are formed from short recent records to help the estimates reflect the current operating condition.</p>\n<p>The spectra are updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> using the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of both signals, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and has a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexing its samples, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 26, "date": "2026-09-24 21:39", "note": "Port unsaved author edits from review page", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Spectral identification of the outer controller loop's closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight applied to the response estimate at each frequency. As the specimen changes during the experiment, these spectra are formed from short recent records to help the estimates reflect the current operating condition.</p>\n<p>The spectra are updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> using the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of both signals, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and has a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexing its samples, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 27, "date": "2026-09-24 21:44", "note": "Improve framework overview and control-loop prose with current writing guide", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">This study extends the NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI) proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] to experimental implementation. The indicator quantifies closed-loop robustness as the physical plant changes during the experiment. Their analysis showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. The experimental framework combines an online estimator with an adaptive controller.</p>\n<p>The estimator forms three margin curves at each frequency. Direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, uses the controller reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. It derives a margin from the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimate [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] of the closed-loop transfer function from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. Output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, can detect loop amplification where direct identification is unreliable by comparing measured output power with a pre-excitation reference. The prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, supplies a fallback when information from measured signals is insufficient. The branches are weighted by confidence in their output before their margins are combined. The adaptive controller uses the resulting indicator to set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which scales the outer controller output. This adjustment seeks an adequate stability margin while retaining as much tracking performance as possible during specimen degradation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>The numerical substructure in Figure 1 computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. A phase-lead compensator advances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to offset transfer-system lag and improve tracking. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> responds to the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is measured actuator displacement. The adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> scales the controller output to form the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant combines the transfer system with the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. Control–structure interaction couples the actuator and specimen and is included in the transfer-system dynamics. Force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to close the numerical substructure loop, while displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to close the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Spectral identification of the outer controller loop's closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight applied to the response estimate at each frequency. As the specimen changes during the experiment, these spectra are formed from short recent records to help the estimates reflect the current operating condition.</p>\n<p>The spectra are updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> using the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of both signals, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and has a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexing its samples, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 28, "date": "2026-09-24 21:47", "note": "Refine Section 2.2 spectral estimation opening and restore surrounding prose", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also used to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight at each frequency. Short recent records help these spectra reflect changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>The spectra are updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Consecutive windows therefore overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and has a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> index the updates and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> index samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 29, "date": "2026-09-24 21:52", "note": "Keep Section 2.2 introduction general; use moving window", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also used to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window keeps these spectra responsive to changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>At each update, the window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates are separated by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> index the updates and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> index samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 30, "date": "2026-09-24 21:55", "note": "Clarify ratios that cancel spectral normalization", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also used to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window keeps these spectra responsive to changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>At each update, the window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates are separated by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> index the updates and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> index samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because their common scale cancels in the transfer-function estimate, coherence, and later power ratios. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 31, "date": "2026-09-24 21:58", "note": "Remove unnecessary spectral normalization sentence", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also used to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window keeps these spectra responsive to changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>At each update, the window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates are separated by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> index the updates and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> index samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}];
window.MANUSCRIPT_WORD = {"eq1": "L(s,λ,k)=λ C(s) H(s,k)", "eq2": "T(s,λ,k)=(Y(s))/(U(s))=(L(s,λ,k))/(1+L(s,λ,k))", "eq3": "b_(\"NCF\") (λ,k)=\"inf\"┬ω  δ(H(jω,k),λ C(jω))", "eq4": "δ(H,λ C)=(|1+λ C H|)/(√(1+|H|^2) √(1+|λ C|^2))", "eq5": "X_n (ω_m)=∑_(r=0)^(N−1)▒〖w[r] x_n [r] e^(−j2πmr/N)〗,  x∈{u,y}", "eq6": "p_(uu,n)=|U_n |^2,  p_(yy,n)=|Y_n |^2,  p_(yu,n)=Y_n U_n^*", "eq7": "p̄_(ab,n) (ω)=(1−η_s) p̄_(ab,n−1) (ω)+η_s p_(ab,n) (ω), ab∈{uu,yy,yu}", "eq8": "Ω={ω_m:2≤f_m≤100 \"Hz\", f_m∉[58,62]\" Hz\"},  f_m=(ω_m)/(2π)", "eq9": "T̂_n (jω)=(p̄_(yu,n) (ω))/(p̄_(uu,n) (ω)),  γ_n^2 (ω)=(|p̄_(yu,n) (ω)|^2)/(p̄_(uu,n) (ω) p̄_(yy,n) (ω))", "eq10": "A_n={■(1,&u_(\"rms\",n)>η_A@0,&\"otherwise\")┤", "eq11": "ℓ_n (ω)=(p_(\"rel\",n) (ω))/(p_(\"rel\",n) (ω)+p_(\"floor\") ),  p_(\"rel\",n) (ω)=(p̄_(uu,n) (ω))/(\"max\"┬(ν∈ℬ)  p̄_(uu,n) (ν))", "eq12": "c_n (ω)=A_n γ_n^2 (ω) ℓ_n (ω), 0≤c_n (ω)≤1", "eq13": "L_(\"pr\",n) (jω)=λ_n L_0 (jω),  T_(\"pr\",n) (jω)=(L_(\"pr\",n) (jω))/(1+L_(\"pr\",n) (jω))", "eq14": "T̃_n (jω)=c_n T̂_n (jω)+(1−c_n ) T_(\"pr\",n) (jω),  L̂_n (jω)=(T̃_n (jω))/(1−T̃_n (jω))", "eq15": "b_(\"id\",n) (ω)=\"med\"_3 {δ(Ĥ_n (jω),λ_n C(jω))},  b_(0,n) (ω)=δ(H_0 (jω),λ_n C(jω))", "eq16": "p_(yy,0) (ω)=\"median\"┬(h=1,2,3)  |Y_h^((0)) (ω)|^2,  q_(\"obs\",n) (ω)=(p̄_(yy,n) (ω))/(p_(yy,0) (ω))", "eq17": "q_(\"mod\",n) (ω,g)=〖|(1+L_0 (jω))/(1+λ_n g L_0 (jω))|〗^2", "eq18": "g_(\"fit\",n) (ω)=\"arg min\"┬(g∈𝒢)  |log q_(\"obs\",n) (ω)−log q_(\"mod\",n) (ω,g)|", "eq19": "log ḡ_n (ω)=(1−η_g) log ḡ_(n−1) (ω)+η_g log g_(\"tar\",n) (ω)", "eq20": "r_(Ω,n)=\"median\"┬(ω∈Ω)  q_(\"obs\",n) (ω)", "eq21": "b_(\"pow\",n) (ω)=\"med\"_3 {δ(ḡ_n (ω) H_0 (jω),λ_n C(jω))}", "eq22": "d_n (ω)=\"clip\"_([0,1]) ((ḡ_n (ω)−1)/(g_(\"det\")−1))", "eq23": "w_(\"id\",n)=c_n,  w_(\"pow\",n)=(1−c_n ) d_n,  w_(0,n)=(1−c_n )(1−d_n )", "eq24": "β_n (ω)=w_(\"id\",n) b_(\"id\",n) (ω)+w_(\"pow\",n) b_(\"pow\",n) (ω)+w_(0,n) b_(0,n) (ω)", "eq25": "b_(\"inst\",n)=β_(n,(2))=β_n (ω_(*,n))", "eq26": "b_(\"OSI\",n)=(1−η_b) b_(\"OSI\",n−1)+η_b b_(\"inst\",n)"};
